var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Macros","page":"API","text":"","title":"Macros"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Hyperoptimizer","page":"API","text":"","title":"Hyperoptimizer"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Sampler-types","page":"API","text":"","title":"Sampler types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Dimension-types","page":"API","text":"","title":"Dimension types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Hyperband-functions","page":"API","text":"","title":"Hyperband functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Optim-extension","page":"API","text":"","title":"Optim extension"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Settings","page":"API","text":"","title":"Settings"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Hyperopt","page":"API","text":"Hyperparameter optimization by random search, Latin hypercube sampling, Hyperband and BOHB.\n\nThe main interface is the macro @hyperopt, which evaluates the body of a for loop for configurations of the hyperparameters and returns a Hyperoptimizer that records the configurations and the results. The documentation is available at https://baggepinnen.github.io/Hyperopt.jl/stable/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Hyperopt.@hyperopt","page":"API","text":"ho = @hyperopt for i = n, [sampler = RandomSampler(),] [ho = existing_ho,] name1 = candidates1, name2 = candidates2, ...\n    loop body\nend\n\nEvaluate the loop body for configurations of the hyperparameters name1, name2, ... drawn from the candidate vectors candidates1, candidates2, ..., and return the Hyperoptimizer ho that records the configurations in ho.history and the results in ho.results. The optimal configuration is obtained with ho.minimizer, see Hyperoptimizer.\n\nThe arguments of the for loop are:\n\ni = n: The first argument. Without Hyperband, n is the number of iterations, i.e., the number of configurations that are drawn and evaluated, and i holds the index of the current iteration. With a Hyperband sampler, n is the maximum budget R, and i holds the budget of the current evaluation. Any variable name can be used instead of i.\nsampler = s (optional): The sampler that draws the configurations: RandomSampler (the default), LHSampler, CLHSampler or Hyperband.\nho = existing_ho (optional): A Hyperoptimizer whose optimization is continued. The configurations and results of the new run are appended to existing_ho.history and existing_ho.results, which the returned Hyperoptimizer shares with existing_ho. The sampler of existing_ho is continued, including the state of its random number generator and, for BOHB, its observations. A sampler given with sampler = s is not used, and a warning is emitted if it is of another type than the sampler of existing_ho.\nname = candidates: One argument per hyperparameter. candidates is a vector of the values that the hyperparameter can take, e.g., LinRange(1, 5, 100), exp10.(LinRange(-3, 1, 100)) for logarithmically spaced values, [true, false] or [tanh, sin]. In the loop body, the variable name holds the value of the hyperparameter.\n\nThe names sampler and ho are reserved and cannot be used as names of hyperparameters.\n\nThe loop body becomes the body of a function whose arguments are the first loop variable and the hyperparameters; variables of the enclosing scope are accessible as in a closure. The keyword argument state of this function is also accessible in the loop body; it is nothing unless the sampler is a Hyperband sampler.\n\nThe value of the loop body is the result of the evaluation:\n\nWithout Hyperband, the result is appended to ho.results. The result is typically a real-valued cost, which ho.minimum and ho.minimizer minimize.\nWith Hyperband, the loop body returns a tuple (loss, state), where state is any object from which a later evaluation of the same configuration with a larger budget can resume, or only the real-valued loss. The loss is appended to ho.results, and the state is passed to the next evaluation of the configuration as the keyword argument state. See Hyperband.\n\nIf an evaluation throws an InterruptException, e.g., after Ctrl-C, the optimization is stopped and ho is returned with the results obtained so far. Other exceptions are rethrown. A progress meter is displayed unless Hyperopt.SHOW_PROGRESS[] is false. The macros @phyperopt and @thyperopt evaluate the loop body in parallel.\n\nExample\n\ncost(a, b, c) = (a - 3)^2 + (b ? 10 : 20) + (log10(c) - 2)^2\nho = @hyperopt for i = 50, sampler = RandomSampler(), a = LinRange(1, 5, 1000), b = [true, false], c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 1000))\n    cost(a, b, c)\nend\nho.minimum, ho.minimizer\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.@hyperopt"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Hyperopt.@phyperopt","page":"API","text":"Same as @hyperopt but uses Distributed.pmap for parallel evaluation of the cost function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.@phyperopt"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Hyperopt.@thyperopt","page":"API","text":"Same as @hyperopt but uses ThreadPools.tmap for multithreaded evaluation of the cost function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.@thyperopt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.Hyperoptimizer","page":"API","text":"Hyperoptimizer(iterations, sampler = RandomSampler(); name1 = candidates1, name2 = candidates2, ...)\nHyperoptimizer(; iterations, params, candidates, history = [], results = [], sampler = RandomSampler(), objective = nothing)\n\nSpecification and record of a hyperparameter optimization. @hyperopt constructs and returns a Hyperoptimizer.\n\nThe first constructor creates a Hyperoptimizer for manual iteration. The keyword arguments name the hyperparameters and give their candidate vectors. Each iteration over the Hyperoptimizer draws a configuration from the sampler, appends it to ho.history and yields it as a NamedTuple (; i, name1, name2, ...), where i is the index of the iteration. The result of each configuration is to be appended to ho.results by the caller:\n\nho = Hyperoptimizer(10, a = LinRange(1, 2, 50), b = [true, false])\nfor (i, a, b) in ho\n    push!(ho.results, cost(a, b))\nend\n\nSee iterate for the semantics of repeated iteration. The second constructor sets the fields directly.\n\nFields\n\niterations::Int: The number of configurations that one run draws, i.e., one execution of @hyperopt or one iteration over the Hyperoptimizer. With a Hyperband sampler, hyperband sets it to the number of configurations that one run of Hyperband draws from the inner sampler.\nparams: The names of the hyperparameters, as symbols.\ncandidates: The candidate vectors, one per hyperparameter, in the order of params.\nhistory: The evaluated configurations. history[k] contains one value per hyperparameter, in the order of params.\nresults: The results of the evaluations. results[k] is the result of the evaluation of the configuration history[k].\nsampler: The sampler, e.g., RandomSampler, LHSampler, CLHSampler or Hyperband.\nobjective: The function that @hyperopt generates from the loop body, called as objective(i, values...; state), or nothing for a Hyperoptimizer that is iterated manually.\n\nProperties\n\nho.minimum: The smallest result.\nho.minimizer: The configuration of the smallest result, an element of ho.history.\nho.maximum: The largest result.\nho.maximizer: The configuration of the largest result, an element of ho.history.\n\nThese properties compare the elements of ho.results. A NaN result, e.g., of an evaluation that failed, is treated as Inf by minimum and minimizer and as -Inf by maximum and maximizer; it is therefore selected only if all compared results are NaN, in which case ho.minimum is Inf and ho.maximum is -Inf. If ho.results is empty, ho.minimum and ho.maximum are NaN, and ho.minimizer and ho.maximizer are empty vectors. With a Hyperband sampler, only the results obtained with the largest budget in ho.sampler.budgets are compared, since results obtained with different budgets are not comparable.\n\nminimum(ho) and maximum(ho) are equivalent to ho.minimum and ho.maximum. printmin and printmax print the minimizer and the maximizer, and warn_on_boundary reports hyperparameters whose optimal value is located on the boundary of the sampled values. If Plots.jl is loaded, plot(ho) plots the results against the value of each hyperparameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.Hyperoptimizer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.iterate-Tuple{Hyperoptimizer}","page":"API","text":"iterate(ho::Hyperoptimizer, state = 1; update_history = true)\n\nDraw the sample of iteration state from the sampler of ho and return it as a NamedTuple with the iteration index i and one entry per parameter, e.g., (i = 1, a = 0.3, b = true).\n\nIf update_history = true, the sample is appended to ho.history; the corresponding result is to be appended to ho.results by the caller:\n\nfor (i, a, b) in ho\n    push!(ho.results, f(a, b))\nend\n\nho.history[k] and ho.results[k] then refer to the same evaluation. Entries at the end of ho.history without a corresponding entry in ho.results, e.g., the samples of an earlier iteration whose results were not recorded, are removed when the iteration starts (state = 1). Iterating over ho again after the results of all samples were recorded appends ho.iterations new samples, which corresponds to continuing the optimization. For LHSampler and CLHSampler, such an iteration evaluates a new plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.iterate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.minimum-Tuple{Hyperoptimizer}","page":"API","text":"minimum(ho::Hyperoptimizer)\n\nReturn the smallest result in ho.results, equivalent to ho.minimum. NaN results are treated as Inf. If ho contains no results, NaN is returned. With a Hyperband sampler, only the results obtained with the largest budget are compared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.minimum"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.maximum-Tuple{Hyperoptimizer}","page":"API","text":"maximum(ho::Hyperoptimizer)\n\nReturn the largest result in ho.results, equivalent to ho.maximum. NaN results are treated as -Inf. If ho contains no results, NaN is returned. With a Hyperband sampler, only the results obtained with the largest budget are compared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.maximum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Hyperopt.printmin","page":"API","text":"printmin([io = stdout,] ho)\n\nPrint the name and the value of each hyperparameter of the configuration ho.minimizer, one hyperparameter per line. With a Hyperband sampler, the configuration is the minimizer among the results obtained with the largest budget, see Hyperoptimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.printmin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Hyperopt.printmax","page":"API","text":"printmax([io = stdout,] ho)\n\nPrint the name and the value of each hyperparameter of the configuration ho.maximizer, one hyperparameter per line. With a Hyperband sampler, the configuration is the maximizer among the results obtained with the largest budget, see Hyperoptimizer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.printmax"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Hyperopt.warn_on_boundary","page":"API","text":"warn_on_boundary(ho, sense = :min)\n\nEmit a warning for each hyperparameter whose value in the optimal configuration is the smallest or the largest value of this hyperparameter in ho.history, and return the names of these hyperparameters as a vector of symbols. The optimal configuration is ho.minimizer if sense = :min, and ho.maximizer otherwise.\n\nExample: if the hyperparameter a has the candidates 1:10 and the optimum is obtained with a = 1, the optimum may be located outside of the candidate range, and the range is to be extended. The warning is also emitted if the smallest sampled value of a is larger than 1 and the optimum is obtained with this value.\n\nOnly hyperparameters whose candidates are real numbers with more than three distinct values are considered, since categorical candidates have no order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.warn_on_boundary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.RandomSampler","page":"API","text":"RandomSampler{T<:AbstractRNG} <: Sampler\n\nSample a value for each parameter uniformly at random from the candidate vectors. Log-uniform sampling available by providing a log-spaced candidate vector.\n\nOptionally, pass an AbstractRNG to initialize. By default, a Xoshiro generator is seeded with a random number drawn from the global RNG, so that Random.seed! makes the samples reproducible.\n\nRandomSampler is the default sampler of @hyperopt and the default inner sampler of Hyperband. The values are drawn independently and with replacement, a configuration may therefore be drawn more than once. The candidate vectors may have different lengths and may contain values of any type, e.g., functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.RandomSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.LHSampler","page":"API","text":"LHSampler(; rng = Xoshiro(rand(UInt)), steps = nothing)\n\nSample from an optimized Latin hypercube plan. The plan contains one point per iteration, and every element of each candidate vector occurs in exactly one point. All candidate vectors must therefore have the same length as the number of iterations. CLHSampler admits categorical hyperparameters with fewer candidates.\n\nThe plan is obtained from a random Latin hypercube by steps proposed exchanges of the entries of two points in one dimension. An exchange is accepted if it reduces the Audze-Eglais potential energy of the plan, i.e., the sum of the inverse squared distances between all pairs of points, in the coordinates given by the candidate indices divided by the number of iterations. The computational cost of a step is proportional to n*d for n iterations and d hyperparameters. The default number of steps, clamp(10^8 ÷ (n*d), n*d, 100*n*d), thus limits the cost to approximately 10^8 evaluations of coordinate differences if n*d ≤ 10^4. With steps = 0, the plan is a random Latin hypercube.\n\nThe plan is drawn from rng. By default, a Xoshiro generator is seeded with a random number drawn from the global RNG, so that Random.seed! makes the plans reproducible.\n\nA new plan is generated for every run of @hyperopt, including a run that resumes an existing Hyperoptimizer with ho = ..., and for every iteration over a Hyperoptimizer that begins after results were recorded. The optimization of a new plan reduces the number of its points that coincide with configurations in ho.history with priority over the potential energy: an exchange that reduces this number is accepted, and an exchange that increases it is rejected. Configurations are thus not evaluated again if the number of steps suffices. With a single hyperparameter, however, every plan consists of all candidates.\n\nAs the inner sampler of Hyperband, the plan contains one point per element of the candidate vectors instead of one point per iteration, see Hyperband.\n\nExample\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100, sampler = LHSampler(), a = LinRange(1, 5, 100), b = repeat([true, false], 50)\n    (a - 3)^2 + b\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.LHSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.CLHSampler","page":"API","text":"CLHSampler(; dims, rng = Xoshiro(rand(UInt)), steps = nothing)\n\nSample from an optimized Latin hypercube plan with continuous and categorical dimensions. dims contains one dimension type per hyperparameter, in the order of the candidate vectors, e.g., dims = [Continuous(), Categorical(2), Continuous()]:\n\nContinuous(): the candidate vector must have the same length as the number of iterations, and every candidate occurs in exactly one point of the plan.\nCategorical(k) or UnorderedCategorical(k): a categorical hyperparameter with k candidates, i.e., the candidate vector must have k elements. In a plan with n points, every candidate occurs in n ÷ k or n ÷ k + 1 points. Both types are treated as unordered: a categorical dimension contributes 1 to the squared distance between two points with different candidates and 0 otherwise. The optimized plan thus distributes the points that share a categorical candidate over the continuous dimensions.\n\nThe keyword arguments rng and steps, and the generation of new plans are described in LHSampler.\n\nExample\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100, sampler = CLHSampler(dims = [Continuous(), Categorical(2)]), a = LinRange(1, 5, 100), b = [true, false]\n    (a - 3)^2 + b\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.CLHSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.Hyperband","page":"API","text":"Hyperband(; R = nothing, η = 3, inner = RandomSampler())\nHyperband(R)\n\nSampler that implements Hyperband: A Novel Bandit-Based Approach to Hyperparameter Optimization. Hyperband runs several brackets of successive halving. In each bracket, configurations drawn from the sampler inner are evaluated with a budget, i.e., an amount of resources. The fraction 1/η of the configurations with the smallest losses is evaluated again with η times the budget, until the maximum budget R is reached.\n\nWith @hyperopt, the first loop argument names the variable that holds the budget of an evaluation, and its value is the maximum budget R, which then does not have to be given to the sampler:\n\nho = @hyperopt for resources = 81, sampler = Hyperband(η = 3), a = LinRange(1, 5, 100), b = [true, false]\n    model = state === nothing ? initial_model(a, b) : state\n    train!(model, resources)\n    loss(model), model\nend\n\nIn every evaluation, the hyperparameter variables hold the configuration. The keyword state is nothing in the first evaluation of a configuration, and otherwise the state returned by the previous evaluation of the same configuration. The loop body returns (loss, state), where the state is any object from which a later evaluation can resume, e.g., the weights of a model and the state of its optimizer. If the loop body returns only the loss, state is nothing in every evaluation. If R is also given to the sampler and differs from the value of the first loop argument, the value of the sampler is used and a deprecation warning is emitted.\n\nThe evaluations are recorded in ho.history (the configurations), ho.results (the losses) and ho.sampler.budgets (the budgets). ho.minimum, ho.minimizer, ho.maximum and ho.maximizer compare only the results obtained with the largest budget. ho.sampler.minimum is nothing before the first evaluation, and otherwise the named tuple (; loss, budget, state, configuration) of the evaluation that ho.minimum refers to. plot(ho) shows the results of each budget as a separate series. ho.iterations is the number of configurations that one run of Hyperband draws from inner. A Hyperband sampler records the evaluations of one Hyperoptimizer and cannot be used for several.\n\nA run that continues an existing Hyperoptimizer with ho = ... runs all brackets again and continues the sampler of the existing Hyperoptimizer, including R, η, the recorded budgets and minimum. The value of the first loop argument of the continued run is not used.\n\nWith inner = LHSampler() or CLHSampler(...), the configurations of all brackets of a run are distinct points of a Latin hypercube plan, which contains one point per element of the candidate vectors. The candidate vectors must therefore contain at least ho.iterations elements.\n\nSee also hyperband for an interface without the macro.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.Hyperband"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.BOHB","page":"API","text":"BOHB(; dims, ρ = 1/3, q = 0.15, N_s = 64, N_min = nothing, bw_factor = 3, min_bandwidth = 1e-3, random_sampler = RandomSampler())\n\nInner sampler of Hyperband that implements the model-based sampling of BOHB: Robust and Efficient Hyperparameter Optimization at Scale. A fraction ρ of the configurations is sampled at random. The remaining configurations are proposed by maximizing the ratio l(x)/g(x) of two kernel density estimates, fitted to the best and the worst configurations evaluated with the largest budget for which sufficiently many observations are available. The observations are the evaluated configurations, i.e., the values of the hyperparameters, and their losses; the states returned by the objective are not used. The observations are recorded by Hyperband; a BOHB sampler that is not the inner sampler of a Hyperband sampler therefore draws only random configurations. A run that continues an existing Hyperoptimizer with ho = ... continues the sampler, including its observations.\n\ndims contains one dimension type per hyperparameter, in the order of the candidate vectors:\n\nContinuous(): an ordered, real-valued hyperparameter. The densities are estimated in the coordinate given by the index into the sorted candidate vector, scaled to [0, 1]. A log-spaced candidate vector thus corresponds to a logarithmic coordinate. Ordered discrete hyperparameters, such as integers, may also be declared Continuous.\nCategorical(n) or UnorderedCategorical(n): a categorical hyperparameter with n candidates. Both types are treated as unordered and use an Aitchison-Aitken kernel.\n\nEvery proposal consists of elements of the candidate vectors.\n\nThe remaining keyword arguments refer to the symbols in the paper:\n\nρ: Fraction of random samples\nq: Fraction of the best observations used to fit the density of good configurations l(x)\nN_s: Number of samples drawn from l(x), of which the one with the largest ratio l(x)/g(x) is proposed\nN_min: Minimum number of observations used to fit each density, the default is the number of hyperparameters plus one\nbw_factor: Factor by which the bandwidths of the continuous dimensions are multiplied when sampling from l(x)\nmin_bandwidth: Lower bound of the bandwidths. This parameter does not occur in the paper but is used in the reference implementation HpBandSter.\nrandom_sampler: RandomSampler used for the random configurations. Its RNG is used for all random decisions of BOHB, a run is thus reproducible with, e.g., random_sampler = RandomSampler(MersenneTwister(0)).\n\nExample\n\nho = @hyperopt for resources = 27, sampler = Hyperband(η = 3, inner = BOHB(dims = [Continuous(), Categorical(2)])), a = LinRange(1, 5, 100), b = [true, false]\n    (a - 3)^2 + b + 1/resources\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.BOHB"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.Continuous","page":"API","text":"Continuous()\n\nAn ordered hyperparameter, e.g., a real-valued or an integer-valued hyperparameter. CLHSampler requires the candidate vector of a Continuous hyperparameter to have the same length as the number of iterations. BOHB requires real-valued candidates and estimates the densities in the coordinate given by the index into the sorted candidate vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.Continuous"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.Categorical","page":"API","text":"Categorical(levels)\n\nA categorical hyperparameter with levels candidates, i.e., its candidate vector has levels elements. CLHSampler and BOHB treat the candidates as unordered, Categorical(levels) is therefore equivalent to UnorderedCategorical(levels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.Categorical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.UnorderedCategorical","page":"API","text":"UnorderedCategorical(levels)\n\nA categorical hyperparameter with levels unordered candidates, e.g., UnorderedCategorical(3) for fun = [tanh, sin, cos]. Equivalent to Categorical(levels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.UnorderedCategorical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Hyperopt.DimensionType","page":"API","text":"DimensionType\n\nAbstract supertype of the types Continuous, Categorical and UnorderedCategorical, which describe the hyperparameters for CLHSampler and BOHB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.DimensionType"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Hyperopt.hyperband","page":"API","text":"hyperband(ho::Hyperoptimizer{Hyperband}; threads = false, mapfun = threads ? ThreadPools.tmap : map)\n\nRun Hyperband with the sampler ho.sampler and the objective ho.objective, which is called as ho.objective(budget, configuration...; state) and returns (loss, state) or only the loss, see Hyperband. The evaluations are appended to ho.history, ho.results and ho.sampler.budgets. mapfun evaluates the configurations of a rung, see Hyperopt.successive_halving.\n\nReturn ho.sampler.minimum.\n\n\n\n\n\nhyperband(f, candidates; R, η = 3, inner = RandomSampler(), threads = false)\n\nRun Hyperband without the macro @hyperopt and return the Hyperoptimizer. candidates is a vector or a NamedTuple of candidate vectors, one per hyperparameter. The objective is called as f(budget, pars), where pars is the vector of hyperparameter values in the first evaluation of a configuration, and the state returned by the previous evaluation of the same configuration otherwise. f returns (loss, state) or only the loss; in the latter case, pars is the vector of hyperparameter values in every evaluation. See Hyperband for the remaining arguments and the recorded results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.hyperband"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Hyperopt.successive_halving","page":"API","text":"successive_halving(ho, n, r = 1, s = round(Int, log(ho.sampler.η, n)); threads = false, mapfun = threads ? ThreadPools.tmap : map)\nsuccessive_halving(ho, n, budgets::AbstractVector; threads = false, mapfun = threads ? ThreadPools.tmap : map)\n\nRun successive halving with the Hyperband sampler ho.sampler. n configurations are drawn from the inner sampler and evaluated with the budget budgets[1]. The fraction 1/η of the configurations with the smallest losses is evaluated again with the budget budgets[2], and so on. The first method uses the budgets r * η^i for i = 0:s.\n\nmapfun(f, configurations, states) evaluates the configurations of a rung and returns the values of f, e.g., map, ThreadPools.tmap or Distributed.pmap. The function f captures only ho.objective and the budget.\n\nReturn ho.sampler.minimum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.successive_halving"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Hyperopt.hyperoptim","page":"API","text":"hyperoptim(f, candidates, algorithm = Optim.NelderMead(), opts = Optim.Options(); R = 30, η = 3, inner = RandomSampler(), threads = true, verbose = false, Rmin = 0.1, kwargs...)\n\nMulti-start optimization with Optim and Hyperband. The interface is similar to that of Optim.optimize, but the initial guess is replaced by candidate vectors, one per element of the initial guess, from which hyperband draws initial guesses. The budget of an evaluation is the time limit in seconds of a call to Optim.optimize. An evaluation with a larger budget continues the optimization from the minimizer found by the previous evaluation of the same initial guess.\n\nReturns the Hyperoptimizer ho. ho.minimum is the smallest value of f found with the largest budget, ho.minimizer is the initial guess from which this value was found, and ho.sampler.minimum.state is the corresponding minimizer of f.\n\nThis function requires Optim.jl to be loaded, i.e., using Optim.\n\nArguments:\n\nf: A function from Rⁿ to R, i.e., it takes a vector of parameters.\ncandidates: A vector or a NamedTuple of candidate vectors, one per element of the vector of parameters.\nalgorithm: One of the Optim algorithms.\nopts: Optim.Options. The field time_limit is replaced by the budget of each call to Optim.optimize.\nR: The maximum budget, i.e., the largest time limit in seconds.\nη: The ratio of the budgets of successive rounds of successive halving, see Hyperband.\ninner: The inner sampler\nthreads: Use threading to evaluate the cost function\nverbose: Print the budget and the minimum after each call to Optim.optimize.\nRmin: The smallest time limit in seconds; smaller budgets are replaced by Rmin.\nkwargs: Are sent to Optim.optimize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.hyperoptim"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#Hyperopt.SHOW_PROGRESS","page":"API","text":"Hyperopt.SHOW_PROGRESS[] = false\n\nDisable the progress meters that @hyperopt, hyperband and hyperoptim display. The progress meters are enabled by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Hyperopt.SHOW_PROGRESS"},{"category":"section","location":"bohb/#BOHB","page":"BOHB","text":"BOHB implements the model-based sampling of BOHB: Robust and Efficient Hyperparameter Optimization at Scale. It is used as the inner sampler of Hyperband and replaces the random sampling of the configurations: a fraction ρ of the configurations is drawn at random, and the remaining configurations are proposed by maximizing the ratio l(x)g(x) of two kernel density estimates, fitted to the best and to the worst configurations evaluated with the largest budget for which sufficiently many observations are available. Before sufficiently many observations are available, all configurations are drawn at random.","title":"BOHB"},{"category":"section","location":"bohb/#Dimension-types","page":"BOHB","text":"The keyword argument dims contains one dimension type per hyperparameter, in the order of the candidate vectors:\n\nContinuous() for ordered, real-valued hyperparameters, including integer-valued ones. The densities are estimated in the coordinate given by the index into the sorted candidate vector, scaled to 0 1. A logarithmically spaced candidate vector, such as exp10.(LinRange(-4, -1, 100)), is thus modeled in a logarithmic coordinate.\nCategorical(k) or, equivalently, UnorderedCategorical(k) for categorical hyperparameters with k candidates, e.g., Categorical(2) for [false, true].\n\nEvery configuration proposed by BOHB consists of elements of the candidate vectors.","title":"Dimension types"},{"category":"section","location":"bohb/#Example","page":"BOHB","text":"The example of the page Hyperband is extended by the categorical hyperparameter nesterov, which selects Nesterov's accelerated gradient instead of gradient descent with momentum:\n\nconst scales = [1.0, 10.0, 100.0]\nloss(w) = sum(scales .* (w .- 1) .^ 2)\ngradient(w) = 2 .* scales .* (w .- 1)\n\nfunction train(w, v, lr, β, nesterov, epochs)\n    for _ in 1:epochs\n        g = nesterov ? gradient(w + β * v) : gradient(w)\n        v = β * v - lr * g\n        w = w + v\n    end\n    w, v\nend\n\nfunction evaluate(lr, β, nesterov, epochs, state)\n    w, v, trained = state === nothing ? (zeros(3), zeros(3), 0) : state\n    w, v = train(w, v, lr, β, nesterov, round(Int, epochs) - trained)\n    loss(w), (w, v, round(Int, epochs))\nend\n\nho = @hyperopt for epochs = 81,\n                   sampler = Hyperband(η = 3, inner = BOHB(dims = [Continuous(), Continuous(), Categorical(2)])),\n                   lr = exp10.(LinRange(-4, -1.5, 100)),\n                   β = LinRange(0, 0.95, 100),\n                   nesterov = [false, true]\n    evaluate(lr, β, nesterov, epochs, state)\nend\nprintmin(ho)\nlr = 0.013219411484660293\nβ = 0.6813131313131312\nnesterov = false\n\nThe configurations proposed by BOHB are evaluated as new configurations, i.e., state is nothing in their first evaluation. The observations of BOHB are the evaluated configurations and their losses; the states returned by the loop body are not used by BOHB. The results are recorded as for any other inner sampler of Hyperband, see Hyperband:\n\nho.minimum, ho.sampler.minimum.budget\n(8.246103158478552e-9, 81.0)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"bohb/#Reproducibility-and-parameters","page":"BOHB","text":"All random decisions of BOHB use the random number generator of its random sampler, a run is thus reproducible with, e.g., BOHB(dims = [Continuous(), Continuous()], random_sampler = RandomSampler(Xoshiro(0))). The observations are retained when an optimization is continued with ho = .... See the docstring of BOHB for the remaining parameters, which correspond to the symbols of the paper.","title":"Reproducibility and parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"optim/#Optim","page":"Optim","text":"Hyperband can be applied to multi-start optimization with Optim.jl: the configurations are initial guesses, and the budget limits the computational effort of a local optimization. A configuration that is evaluated again with a larger budget continues the optimization from the minimizer found by its previous evaluation.","title":"Optim"},{"category":"section","location":"optim/#hyperoptim","page":"Optim","text":"hyperoptim implements this approach with a time limit in seconds as the budget. It is defined in a package extension and requires Optim.jl to be loaded. The interface is similar to that of Optim.optimize, but the initial guess is replaced by candidate vectors, one per element of the initial guess. The following example minimizes the Rastrigin function, which has many local minima and its global minimum at the origin:\n\nusing Optim\nrastrigin(x) = 10length(x) + sum(xi -> xi^2 - 10cos(2π * xi), x)\n\ncandidates = [LinRange(-5, 5, 100), LinRange(-5, 5, 100)]\nho = hyperoptim(rastrigin, candidates, NelderMead(); R = 9, η = 3)\nho.minimum\n1.0636540537234396e-9\n\nho.minimizer is the initial guess from which the smallest value was found, and ho.sampler.minimum.state is the corresponding minimizer of the function:\n\nho.minimizer, ho.sampler.minimum.state\n([4.595959595959595, 4.292929292929292], [1.2556256315049907e-7, -2.312057358513712e-6])\n\nMost evaluations terminate before the time limit, since Optim.optimize stops when its convergence criteria are satisfied.","title":"hyperoptim"},{"category":"section","location":"optim/#@hyperopt-with-Optim","page":"Optim","text":"The budget can also be another quantity, e.g., the number of iterations. In the following example, the loop body is a local optimization with at most iterations iterations, and the hyperparameters are the initial guess x1, x2 and the optimization algorithm. The state is the minimizer, from which a later evaluation continues the optimization:\n\nho = @hyperopt for iterations = 81,\n                   sampler = Hyperband(η = 3),\n                   algorithm = [NelderMead(), BFGS(), SimulatedAnnealing()],\n                   x1 = LinRange(-5, 5, 100),\n                   x2 = LinRange(-5, 5, 100)\n    x0 = state === nothing ? [x1, x2] : state\n    res = Optim.optimize(rastrigin, x0, algorithm, Optim.Options(iterations = round(Int, iterations)))\n    Optim.minimum(res), Optim.minimizer(res)\nend\nalgorithm, x1, x2 = ho.minimizer\nnameof(typeof(algorithm)), x1, x2\n(:BFGS, -0.6565656565656566, 0.7575757575757578)\n\nThe minimum and the corresponding minimizer of the function are\n\nho.minimum, ho.sampler.minimum.state\n(0.0, [-5.870402146771426e-13, 1.0675378431419373e-13])\n\nIn every evaluation, the hyperparameter variables hold the configuration, i.e., the initial guess and the algorithm of the first evaluation, while state holds the minimizer of the previous evaluation.","title":"@hyperopt with Optim"},{"category":"section","location":"samplers/#Samplers","page":"Samplers","text":"A sampler determines the configurations that @hyperopt evaluates. It is selected with the argument sampler of @hyperopt. The following samplers draw the configurations of a fixed number of iterations:\n\nSampler Candidate vectors Configurations\nRandomSampler Any length, any element type Drawn independently and with replacement\nLHSampler Length equal to the number of iterations Optimized Latin hypercube plan\nCLHSampler Length equal to the number of iterations for Continuous hyperparameters, any length for Categorical hyperparameters Optimized Latin hypercube plan with categorical dimensions\n\nHyperband is a sampler that evaluates configurations drawn from one of these samplers, or from BOHB, with different budgets, see Hyperband.\n\nThe examples on this page use the following cost function of the hyperparameters a, b and c:\n\nf(a, b, c) = (a - 3)^2 + (b ? 0 : 1) + (log10(c) - 1)^2\nf (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Samplers"},{"category":"section","location":"samplers/#Random-sampling","page":"Samplers","text":"RandomSampler draws the value of each hyperparameter uniformly at random from its candidate vector. It is the default sampler.\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100,\n                   sampler = RandomSampler(),\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 50),\n                   b = [true, false],\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 50))\n    f(a, b, c)\nend\nHyperoptimizer with\n  a length: 50\n  b length: Bool[1, 0]\n  c length: 50\n  minimum / maximum: (0.13660974593919198, 8.080383173677635)\n  minimizer:\n        a         b         c \n    2.959         1      23.3 \n\n\nBy default, the random number generator of a RandomSampler is seeded from the global random number generator, so that Random.seed! makes the configurations reproducible. A random number generator can also be given explicitly, e.g., RandomSampler(Xoshiro(0)).","title":"Random sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"samplers/#Latin-hypercube-sampling","page":"Samplers","text":"LHSampler evaluates an optimized Latin hypercube plan, in which every element of each candidate vector occurs in exactly one configuration. All candidate vectors must therefore have the same length as the number of iterations; the candidates of b below are repeated to obtain the required length:\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100,\n                   sampler = LHSampler(),\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 100),\n                   b = repeat([true, false], 50),\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 100))\n    f(a, b, c)\nend\nHyperoptimizer with\n  a length: 100\n  b length: 100\n  c length: 100\n  minimum / maximum: (0.0824405672890522, 6.355371900826444)\n  minimizer:\n        a         b         c \n    3.222         1     6.579 \n\n\nThe plan is optimized such that the configurations are distributed evenly over the candidate values. The following figure compares the configurations of a random search and of a Latin hypercube plan with 30 iterations:\n\nusing Plots\ncandidates_a = LinRange(0, 1, 30)\ncandidates_c = LinRange(0, 1, 30)\nho_random = @hyperopt for i = 30, sampler = RandomSampler(), a = candidates_a, c = candidates_c\n    0.0\nend\nho_lhs = @hyperopt for i = 30, sampler = LHSampler(), a = candidates_a, c = candidates_c\n    0.0\nend\np1 = scatter(first.(ho_random.history), last.(ho_random.history), title = \"RandomSampler\")\np2 = scatter(first.(ho_lhs.history), last.(ho_lhs.history), title = \"LHSampler\")\nplot(p1, p2, xlabel = \"a\", ylabel = \"c\", legend = false, aspect_ratio = :equal, size = (700, 350))\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe keyword argument rng determines the random number generator from which the plan is drawn, e.g., LHSampler(rng = Xoshiro(0)) for a reproducible plan, and steps determines the number of proposed exchanges in the optimization of the plan, see LHSampler.","title":"Latin hypercube sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"samplers/#Latin-hypercube-sampling-with-categorical-hyperparameters","page":"Samplers","text":"CLHSampler admits categorical hyperparameters with fewer candidates than the number of iterations. The keyword argument dims contains one dimension type per hyperparameter: Continuous() for a hyperparameter whose candidate vector has the same length as the number of iterations, and Categorical(k) or, equivalently, UnorderedCategorical(k) for a categorical hyperparameter with k candidates:\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100,\n                   sampler = CLHSampler(dims = [Continuous(), Categorical(2), Continuous()]),\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 100),\n                   b = [true, false],\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 100))\n    f(a, b, c)\nend\ncount(h -> h[2], ho.history) # The number of configurations with b = true\n50\n\nEach candidate of b occurs in 50 configurations, and the plan distributes the configurations with b = true, as well as those with b = false, over the ranges of a and c. The categorical candidates are treated as unordered.","title":"Latin hypercube sampling with categorical hyperparameters"},{"category":"section","location":"samplers/#New-plans-in-continued-optimizations","page":"Samplers","text":"A run that continues an existing Hyperoptimizer with ho = ... continues its sampler. A RandomSampler continues its random number generator. LHSampler and CLHSampler evaluate a new plan in every run, and the optimization of the new plan avoids configurations that were evaluated before:\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100, ho = ho,\n                   sampler = CLHSampler(dims = [Continuous(), Categorical(2), Continuous()]),\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 100),\n                   b = [true, false],\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 100))\n    f(a, b, c)\nend\nlength(ho.history), length(unique(ho.history))\n(200, 200)\n\nThe same applies to an iteration over a Hyperoptimizer that begins after results were recorded, see Manual iteration.","title":"New plans in continued optimizations"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"Hyperopt defines a plot recipe for Hyperoptimizer, which Plots.jl uses for plot(ho). The plot contains one subplot per hyperparameter, which shows the results against the values of the hyperparameter. The plot indicates which hyperparameters have a large effect on the results, and whether the optimum is located at the boundary of the candidate range.\n\nA logarithmic axis is used for the results and for a hyperparameter if all values are positive and the ratio of the smallest to the largest value is less than 2 cdot 10^-2.\nThe values of a hyperparameter that is not real-valued, e.g., a function, are shown as categories.\nResults that are NaN or infinite are not shown.\nFor a Hyperoptimizer with a Hyperband sampler, the results of each budget are shown as a separate series.\n\nThe documentation is built with the GR backend of Plots.jl. On a computer without a display, e.g., in continuous integration, GR is used in headless mode by setting ENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" before Plots.jl is loaded.\n\nPlot attributes given to plot, e.g., layout and size, take precedence over the defaults of the recipe.","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Random-search","page":"Plotting","text":"using Plots\nf(a, b, c) = (a - 3)^2 + (b ? 10 : 20) + (log10(c) - 2)^2\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 200,\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 1000),\n                   b = [true, false],\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 1000))\n    f(a, b, c)\nend\nplot(ho, layout = (1, 3), size = (900, 300), margin = 5Plots.mm)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Random search"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Categorical-hyperparameters","page":"Plotting","text":"ho = @hyperopt for i = 100, fun = [tanh, sin, cos], x = LinRange(0, 1, 100)\n    fun(x) + (x - 0.5)^2\nend\nplot(ho, size = (700, 300), margin = 5Plots.mm)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Categorical hyperparameters"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Hyperband-results","page":"Plotting","text":"The loss in the following example decreases with the budget, and the loss obtained with a small budget indicates the loss obtained with the largest budget:\n\nho = @hyperopt for budget = 81, sampler = Hyperband(η = 3), a = LinRange(1, 5, 100), c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 100))\n    (a - 3)^2 + (log10(c) - 2)^2 + 10 / budget\nend\nplot(ho, size = (800, 300), margin = 5Plots.mm)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Hyperband results"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#Hyperband","page":"Hyperband","text":"Hyperband implements Hyperband: A Novel Bandit-Based Approach to Hyperparameter Optimization. Hyperband evaluates configurations of the hyperparameters with a small budget, i.e., an amount of resources, and evaluates the configurations with the smallest losses again with a larger budget. The interpretation of the budget is determined by the user, e.g., the number of training epochs, a time limit or the number of iterations of an optimizer.","title":"Hyperband"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#Algorithm","page":"Hyperband","text":"Hyperband has two parameters: the maximum budget R and the ratio eta of the budgets of successive rounds. With s_max = lfloor log_eta R rfloor, Hyperband runs the brackets s = s_max s_max-1 dots 0. Bracket s performs successive halving:\n\nn_s = lceil fracs_max+1s+1 eta^s rceil configurations are drawn from the inner sampler and evaluated with the budget R eta^-s.\nIn round i = 1 dots s, the lfloor n_s eta^-i rfloor configurations with the smallest losses of the previous round are evaluated again with the budget R eta^i-s.\n\nThe bracket s = s_max thus evaluates many configurations with the smallest budget, and the bracket s = 0 evaluates few configurations with the maximum budget only.","title":"Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#Usage-with-@hyperopt","page":"Hyperband","text":"With a Hyperband sampler, the first argument of the for loop names the variable that holds the budget of the current evaluation, and its value is the maximum budget R. The parameter eta and the inner sampler, which draws the configurations, are arguments of the sampler: Hyperband(η = 3, inner = RandomSampler()).\n\nA configuration that is evaluated again with a larger budget can resume from the result of its previous evaluation, e.g., continue the training of a model instead of training a new model. The loop body therefore returns a tuple (loss, state), where state is any object from which a later evaluation can resume. In the next evaluation of the same configuration, the variable state holds this object; in the first evaluation of a configuration, state is nothing. If the loop body returns only the loss, state is nothing in every evaluation.\n\nIn the following example, a model with the weights w is trained by gradient descent with momentum. The hyperparameters are the learning rate lr and the momentum coefficient β, and the budget is the number of training epochs. The state contains the weights, the velocity and the number of epochs trained so far. Configurations with a large learning rate diverge, i.e., their loss increases with the number of epochs.\n\nconst scales = [1.0, 10.0, 100.0]\nloss(w) = sum(scales .* (w .- 1) .^ 2)\ngradient(w) = 2 .* scales .* (w .- 1)\n\n# Gradient descent with momentum, starting from the weights w and the velocity v\nfunction train(w, v, lr, β, epochs)\n    for _ in 1:epochs\n        v = β * v - lr * gradient(w)\n        w = w + v\n    end\n    w, v\nend\n\nho = @hyperopt for epochs = 81,\n                   sampler = Hyperband(η = 3, inner = RandomSampler()),\n                   lr = exp10.(LinRange(-4, -1.5, 100)),\n                   β = LinRange(0, 0.95, 100)\n    if state === nothing # The first evaluation of the configuration (lr, β)\n        w, v, trained = zeros(3), zeros(3), 0\n    else                 # Continue the training of the previous evaluation\n        w, v, trained = state\n    end\n    w, v = train(w, v, lr, β, round(Int, epochs) - trained)\n    loss(w), (w, v, round(Int, epochs))\nend","title":"Usage with @hyperopt"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#Recorded-evaluations","page":"Hyperband","text":"The Hyperoptimizer records the configuration, the loss and the budget of each evaluation in ho.history, ho.results and ho.sampler.budgets, respectively. A configuration that is evaluated with several budgets occurs several times in ho.history. The number of evaluations with each budget is\n\nbudgets = ho.sampler.budgets\n[budget => count(==(budget), budgets) for budget in sort(unique(budgets))]\n5-element Vector{Pair{Float64, Int64}}:\n  1.0 => 81\n  3.0 => 61\n  9.0 => 35\n 27.0 => 19\n 81.0 => 10\n\nSince losses obtained with different budgets are not comparable, ho.minimum, ho.minimizer, ho.maximum, ho.maximizer and printmin consider only the evaluations with the largest budget:\n\nho.minimum, ho.minimizer\n(0.0004910230567999196, [0.011103363181676384, 0.49898989898989904])\n\nho.sampler.minimum is the named tuple (; loss, budget, state, configuration) of the evaluation that attains ho.minimum. Its state contains the trained weights:\n\nbest = ho.sampler.minimum\nbest.budget, best.configuration, best.state[1]\n(81.0, [0.011103363181676384, 0.49898989898989904], [0.9778409599305403, 1.0000000000004432, 1.000000000000401])\n\nplot(ho) shows the results of each budget as a separate series, see Plotting.","title":"Recorded evaluations"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#Continuing-a-Hyperband-optimization","page":"Hyperband","text":"The argument ho = ho continues the optimization: all brackets are run again with new configurations, and the evaluations are appended to ho.history, ho.results and ho.sampler.budgets. The sampler of the existing Hyperoptimizer is continued, including its maximum budget and ho.sampler.minimum. For brevity, the loop body of the example above is moved into a function:\n\nfunction evaluate(lr, β, epochs, state)\n    w, v, trained = state === nothing ? (zeros(3), zeros(3), 0) : state\n    w, v = train(w, v, lr, β, round(Int, epochs) - trained)\n    loss(w), (w, v, round(Int, epochs))\nend\n\nn = length(ho.results)\nho = @hyperopt for epochs = 81, ho = ho,\n                   sampler = Hyperband(η = 3, inner = RandomSampler()),\n                   lr = exp10.(LinRange(-4, -1.5, 100)),\n                   β = LinRange(0, 0.95, 100)\n    evaluate(lr, β, epochs, state)\nend\nn, length(ho.results), ho.minimum\n(206, 412, 2.864285702280609e-8)","title":"Continuing a Hyperband optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#Inner-samplers","page":"Hyperband","text":"The configurations are drawn from the inner sampler, RandomSampler by default. With LHSampler or CLHSampler, the configurations of all brackets of a run are distinct points of a Latin hypercube plan, which contains one point per element of the candidate vectors. The candidate vectors must therefore contain at least as many elements as a run draws configurations, which is ho.iterations after the run; otherwise, an error that states the required number is thrown. With R = 27 and eta = 3, a run draws 49 configurations:\n\nho_lhs = @hyperopt for epochs = 27,\n                       sampler = Hyperband(η = 3, inner = LHSampler()),\n                       lr = exp10.(LinRange(-4, -1.5, 100)),\n                       β = LinRange(0, 0.95, 100)\n    evaluate(lr, β, epochs, state)\nend\nho_lhs.iterations, length(unique(ho_lhs.history))\n(49, 49)\n\nBOHB is an inner sampler that proposes configurations based on the losses of the evaluated configurations, see BOHB.","title":"Inner samplers"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperband/#The-maximum-budget","page":"Hyperband","text":"The maximum budget is the value of the first loop argument. It can also be given to the sampler, Hyperband(R = 81, η = 3), in which case the value of the first loop argument is to be equal to R. If the two values differ, R of the sampler is used and a deprecation warning is emitted. In versions before v0.6, the value of the first loop argument was ignored.\n\nSee Function interface for hyperband, which runs Hyperband without the macro.","title":"The maximum budget"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#The-@hyperopt-macro","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"","title":"The @hyperopt macro"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Syntax","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"ho = @hyperopt for i = n,\n                   sampler = RandomSampler(),\n                   ho = existing_ho,\n                   a = candidates_a,\n                   b = candidates_b\n    loop body\nend\n\nThe first argument of the for loop, i = n, determines the number of iterations n, i.e., the number of configurations that are drawn and evaluated. In the loop body, i holds the index of the current iteration. With a Hyperband sampler, n is instead the maximum budget, and i holds the budget of the current evaluation, see Hyperband.\nThe optional argument sampler selects the sampler that draws the configurations. The default is RandomSampler(), see Samplers.\nThe optional argument ho continues the optimization of an existing Hyperoptimizer, see Continuing an optimization.\nThe remaining arguments name the hyperparameters and give their candidate vectors. In the loop body, the variables a and b hold the values of the hyperparameters of the current configuration. A logarithmically spaced candidate vector, e.g., exp10.(LinRange(-3, 1, 100)), results in log-uniform sampling.\n\nThe value of the loop body is the result of the evaluation, typically a real-valued cost that is to be minimized. The loop body is the body of a function, and the variables of the enclosing scope are accessible as in a closure. The variable state is defined in the loop body; it is nothing unless the sampler is a Hyperband sampler. See the docstring of @hyperopt for details.","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Example","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"The function f is minimized with respect to the hyperparameters a, b and c. The candidate vector of c is logarithmically spaced.\n\nf(x, a, b = true; c = 10) = sum(@. x + (a - 3)^2 + (b ? 10 : 20) + (c - 100)^2)\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 50,\n                   sampler = RandomSampler(),\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 1000),\n                   b = [true, false],\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 1000))\n    x = 100\n    f(x, a, b; c)\nend\nHyperoptimizer with\n  a length: 1000\n  b length: Bool[1, 0]\n  c length: 1000\n  minimum / maximum: (703.0463883220827, 701520.0901880633)\n  minimizer:\n        a         b         c \n    2.153         1     75.66 \n","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Results","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"The Hyperoptimizer ho records the evaluated configurations in ho.history and the results in ho.results; ho.results[k] is the result of the configuration ho.history[k]:\n\nho.history[1], ho.results[1]\n(Real[3.6346346346346348, false, 8.588828559546247], 8476.405025235506)\n\nThe properties minimum, minimizer, maximum and maximizer provide the optimal result and configuration:\n\nho.minimum, ho.minimizer\n(703.0463883220827, Real[2.153153153153153, true, 75.66218500481055])\n\nprintmin and printmax print the optimal configuration together with the names of the hyperparameters:\n\nprintmin(ho)\na = 2.153153153153153\nb = true\nc = 75.66218500481055\n\nA NaN result, e.g., of an evaluation that failed, is treated as Inf by minimum and minimizer and as -Inf by maximum and maximizer. It is therefore not selected unless all results are NaN:\n\nho_nan = @hyperopt for i = 20, a = LinRange(-1, 1, 21)\n    a < 0 ? NaN : a^2\nend\nho_nan.minimum, ho_nan.maximum\n(0.0, 1.0)\n\nIf no result has been recorded, ho.minimum and ho.maximum are NaN.\n\nwarn_on_boundary emits a warning for each hyperparameter whose optimal value is the smallest or the largest sampled value, which indicates that the candidate range is to be extended. In the following example, the cost is minimized by a = 3, which is outside of the candidate range:\n\nho_boundary = @hyperopt for i = 30, a = LinRange(0, 2, 100)\n    (a - 3)^2\nend\nboundary_parameters = warn_on_boundary(ho_boundary)\n┌ Warning: Parameter a obtained its optimum on an extremum of the sampled region: 1.97979797979798\n└ @ Hyperopt ~/work/Hyperopt.jl/Hyperopt.jl/src/Hyperopt.jl:577\n\nThe names of these hyperparameters are returned:\n\nboundary_parameters\n1-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :a","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Continuing-an-optimization","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"The argument ho = ho continues the optimization of an existing Hyperoptimizer. The configurations and results of the new run are appended to ho.history and ho.results, and the sampler of ho is continued, including the state of its random number generator:\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 20, ho = ho,\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 1000),\n                   b = [true, false],\n                   c = exp10.(LinRange(-1, 3, 1000))\n    f(100, a, b; c)\nend\nlength(ho.history), length(ho.results)\n(70, 70)\n\nThe returned Hyperoptimizer shares history and results with the existing one. A sampler given in the continued run is not used; a warning is emitted if it is of another type than the sampler of the existing Hyperoptimizer.","title":"Continuing an optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Categorical-hyperparameters","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"The candidates of a hyperparameter can be values of any type, e.g., functions or symbols. RandomSampler draws them like numerical values:\n\nho_cat = @hyperopt for i = 30, fun = [tanh, sin, cos], x = LinRange(0, 1, 50)\n    fun(x) + (x - 0.5)^2\nend\nprintmin(ho_cat)\nfun = tanh\nx = 0.02040816326530612\n\nCLHSampler and BOHB require the categorical hyperparameters to be declared with Categorical or UnorderedCategorical, see Samplers and BOHB.","title":"Categorical hyperparameters"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Interruption-and-errors","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"If an evaluation throws an InterruptException, e.g., after Ctrl-C, the optimization is stopped and the Hyperoptimizer with the results obtained so far is returned. Other exceptions are rethrown. If the optimization of an existing Hyperoptimizer was continued, its history then contains only the configurations whose evaluation completed, so that ho.history and ho.results have equal lengths. An evaluation that is expected to fail for some configurations can return NaN instead, e.g., by\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 100, a = LinRange(1, 5, 100)\n    try\n        train_model(a)\n    catch\n        NaN\n    end\nend","title":"Interruption and errors"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Progress-meters","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"@hyperopt, hyperband and hyperoptim display a progress meter. The progress meters are disabled by\n\nHyperopt.SHOW_PROGRESS[] = false","title":"Progress meters"},{"category":"section","location":"hyperopt/#Parallel-evaluation","page":"The @hyperopt macro","text":"The macros @phyperopt and @thyperopt accept the same syntax as @hyperopt and evaluate the loop body in parallel, on the worker processes of the Distributed standard library and on threads, respectively. See their docstrings for details.","title":"Parallel evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"function_interface/#Function-interface","page":"Function interface","text":"The macro @hyperopt generates a function from the loop body and evaluates it. This page describes two interfaces without the macro: the manual iteration over a Hyperoptimizer, and the function hyperband.","title":"Function interface"},{"category":"section","location":"function_interface/#Manual-iteration","page":"Function interface","text":"A Hyperoptimizer is constructed with the number of iterations, optionally a sampler, and one keyword argument per hyperparameter, which gives its candidate vector. Iterating over the Hyperoptimizer yields one configuration per iteration, as a NamedTuple that contains the index of the iteration i and the values of the hyperparameters:\n\nho = Hyperoptimizer(10, a = LinRange(1, 2, 50), b = [true, false], c = randn(100))\nfor (i, a, b, c) in ho\n    println(i, \"\\t\", a, \"\\t\", b, \"\\t\", c)\nend\n1\t1.7959183673469388\ttrue\t-1.3819748518823003\n2\t1.0408163265306123\tfalse\t-1.2601642953877346\n3\t1.5306122448979593\ttrue\t-0.29415075749027164\n4\t1.5714285714285714\tfalse\t0.27261968870626063\n5\t2.0\tfalse\t-1.0484721702319963\n6\t1.4489795918367347\ttrue\t-1.2601642953877346\n7\t1.306122448979592\tfalse\t-0.9639451905647899\n8\t1.2653061224489797\tfalse\t0.5583656057835097\n9\t1.0612244897959184\ttrue\t-0.5424504046441639\n10\t1.6122448979591837\tfalse\t-0.6705231877296826\n\nEach iteration appends the configuration to ho.history. The results are not recorded by the iteration; to record them, the result of each configuration is appended to ho.results:\n\ncost(a, b, c) = (a - 1.5)^2 + b + c^2\n\nho = Hyperoptimizer(10, a = LinRange(1, 2, 50), b = [true, false], c = randn(100))\nfor (i, a, b, c) in ho\n    push!(ho.results, cost(a, b, c))\nend\nho.minimum, ho.minimizer\n(0.12158615644984314, Real[1.306122448979592, false, -0.28982348362438165])\n\nho.history[k] and ho.results[k] then refer to the same evaluation. When an iteration begins, the configurations at the end of ho.history without a corresponding result are removed, so that repeated iterations do not accumulate configurations that were never evaluated. An iteration that begins after the results of all configurations were recorded appends ho.iterations new configurations, which continues the optimization:\n\nfor (i, a, b, c) in ho\n    push!(ho.results, cost(a, b, c))\nend\nlength(ho.history), length(ho.results)\n(20, 20)\n\nThe configurations can also be collected first and evaluated later, e.g., by an external program:\n\nho = Hyperoptimizer(4, LHSampler(), a = LinRange(1, 2, 4), b = [true, false, true, false])\nconfigurations = collect(ho)\n4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{i::Int64, a::Float64, b::Bool}}:\n (i = 1, a = 1.0, b = 0)\n (i = 2, a = 1.6666666666666665, b = 1)\n (i = 3, a = 1.3333333333333335, b = 0)\n (i = 4, a = 2.0, b = 1)\n\nappend!(ho.results, [cost(x.a, x.b, 0.0) for x in configurations])\nprintmin(ho)\na = 1.3333333333333335\nb = false\n\nFor LHSampler and CLHSampler, an iteration that begins after results were recorded evaluates a new plan.","title":"Manual iteration"},{"category":"section","location":"function_interface/#Hyperband-without-the-macro","page":"Function interface","text":"hyperband runs Hyperband with an objective function f(budget, x) and returns the Hyperoptimizer. x is the vector of hyperparameter values in the first evaluation of a configuration, and the state returned by the previous evaluation of the same configuration otherwise. The objective returns (loss, state), or only the loss, in which case x is the vector of hyperparameter values in every evaluation. The candidate vectors are given as a vector or as a NamedTuple; a NamedTuple names the hyperparameters, e.g., in plots.\n\nIn the following example, the state is a NamedTuple that contains the hyperparameters, the weights of the model and the number of epochs trained so far:\n\nconst scales = [1.0, 10.0, 100.0]\nloss(w) = sum(scales .* (w .- 1) .^ 2)\ngradient(w) = 2 .* scales .* (w .- 1)\n\nfunction objective(epochs, x)\n    s = x isa NamedTuple ? x : (lr = x[1], β = x[2], w = zeros(3), v = zeros(3), trained = 0)\n    w, v = s.w, s.v\n    for _ in 1:(round(Int, epochs) - s.trained)\n        v = s.β * v - s.lr * gradient(w)\n        w = w + v\n    end\n    loss(w), (; s.lr, s.β, w, v, trained = round(Int, epochs))\nend\n\ncandidates = (lr = exp10.(LinRange(-4, -1.5, 100)), β = LinRange(0, 0.95, 100))\nho = hyperband(objective, candidates; R = 81, η = 3)\nho.minimum, ho.minimizer\n(0.00016440226720563258, [0.005857020818056668, 0.8444444444444443])\n\nAs with the macro, ho.sampler.minimum contains the state of the best evaluation with the largest budget:\n\nho.sampler.minimum.state.w\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.9992091489592047\n 1.001059129452811\n 1.001235148856854\n\nThe keyword argument inner selects the inner sampler, e.g., inner = LHSampler() or inner = BOHB(dims = [Continuous(), Continuous()]). See hyperoptim and Optim for an application of hyperband to multi-start optimization.","title":"Hyperband without the macro"},{"category":"section","location":"#Hyperopt.jl","page":"Home","text":"Hyperopt.jl is a package for hyperparameter optimization in Julia. It provides the following methods:\n\nRandom search, RandomSampler.\nLatin hypercube sampling with continuous hyperparameters, LHSampler, and with continuous and categorical hyperparameters, CLHSampler.\nHyperband, Hyperband, which evaluates many configurations with a small budget and evaluates the most promising configurations again with larger budgets.\nBOHB, BOHB, which replaces the random sampling of Hyperband by sampling from kernel density estimates fitted to the evaluated configurations.\n\nThe package is designed to add hyperparameter optimization to existing code. The macro @hyperopt is wrapped around the code that evaluates a configuration of the hyperparameters, and the user specifies the names of the hyperparameters and their candidate values.","title":"Hyperopt.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"Hyperopt\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Consider code that trains a model with the manually selected hyperparameters a and b:\n\na = manually_selected_value\nb = other_value\ncost = train_model(a, b)\n\nTo optimize a and b, the code is wrapped in @hyperopt, and the manually selected values are replaced by vectors of candidate values:\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = number_of_samples,\n                   a = candidate_values,\n                   b = other_candidate_values\n    cost = train_model(a, b)\nend\n\nThe following example evaluates 50 configurations drawn at random:\n\nusing Hyperopt\ntrain_model(a, b) = (a - 3)^2 + (b ? 0.0 : 1.0)\n\nho = @hyperopt for i = 50,\n                   a = LinRange(1, 5, 100),\n                   b = [true, false]\n    cost = train_model(a, b)\nend\nHyperoptimizer with\n  a length: 100\n  b length: Bool[1, 0]\n  minimum / maximum: (0.0004081216202428376, 4.379655137230895)\n  minimizer:\n        a         b \n     3.02         1 \n\n\nThe returned Hyperoptimizer records the configurations and the results, and provides the best configuration:\n\nho.minimizer, ho.minimum\n(Real[3.0202020202020203, true], 0.0004081216202428376)\n\nprintmin(ho)\na = 3.0202020202020203\nb = true","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Which-sampler-to-use?","page":"Home","text":"RandomSampler is the default sampler and a robust baseline. It supports candidate vectors of any length and any element type.\nLHSampler and CLHSampler distribute the configurations evenly over the candidate values. They are preferable to random search if the number of iterations is small, but they require candidate vectors whose lengths are related to the number of iterations, see Samplers.\nHyperband is preferable if the cost of an evaluation is determined by a budget, e.g., the number of training epochs or a time limit, and the result obtained with a small budget indicates the result obtained with a large budget.\nBOHB is used as the inner sampler of Hyperband. It proposes configurations based on the results of the configurations evaluated before.","title":"Which sampler to use?"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"hyperopt.md\", \"samplers.md\", \"hyperband.md\", \"bohb.md\", \"function_interface.md\", \"optim.md\", \"plotting.md\", \"api.md\"]\nDepth = 1","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"If you use Hyperopt.jl in academic work, please cite it using the entry in CITATION.bib:\n\n@misc{bagge2018hyperopt,\n  title        = {Hyperopt.jl: Hyperparameter optimization in {J}ulia},\n  author       = {Bagge Carlson, Fredrik},\n  year         = {2018},\n  month        = aug,\n  howpublished = {\\url{https://github.com/baggepinnen/Hyperopt.jl}},\n  url          = {https://github.com/baggepinnen/Hyperopt.jl},\n  note         = {Lund University Publications, \\url{https://lup.lub.lu.se/record/6ec19989-9b30-448c-be5e-bae4c4257c7b}}\n}","title":"Citing"}]
}
